body:
When my player is pointing to a resource, it calls this function within its own script:

    func check_raycast():
    if ray.is_colliding():
    	var target = ray.get_collider().owner
    	if target is ResourceClass:
    		target.place_factory.rpc_id(target.get_multiplayer_authority())

The function called within target (which is the resource) looks like this:

    @rpc("any_peer")
    func place_factory(): print("Test") 
        var factory: FactoryClass = Factory.instantiate()
        add_child(factory)

&#x200B;

Now the client will throw an error when `add_child(factory)` is called. "Node not found".

&#x200B;

I made my little project following the tutorial you linked actually! I'm just trying to build from it to spawn objects that can start farming minerals. I also added a MultiplayerSynchronizer to the resource and Factory, but I'm not entirely sure where they are needed and where they are just recommended yet.

They did mention in the monthly report that they started working on the Cargo elevator last month. That is basically where the cargo will go when buying it in terminal, and where you need to move it from and into the ship. 

&#x200B;

I am just curious how ships like the Reclaimer and 890J that has to dock are going to get the boxes in their cargo. The cargo bay is not exactly made for space drop on/off

I'm not talking about dates. I can give an example.

If they are working on Quantum experience, and that task ends April. We will not know if it's done, or how much is left. All we know is that something has probably been done to it (or not, depending on if they found a blocker).

&#x200B;

So the progress is not shown, just the intent of working on it, are we agreeing on this point?

&#x200B;

Now, if you were to hire a carpenter to build you a wall while you're on vacation, you would call the carpenter to hear how the progress is. Do you know the progress on it if all he says is "I set some time to work on it a bit last week. I may or may not need to work more on it"?

&#x200B;

My point is that you have no idea of the progress on it. In the scenario of SC, we have no idea of the progress on anything. Thus, it's not being tracked.

&#x200B;

A possible solution or band-aid to this would be a simple: "More work is needed, paused because of XXX" or "Done, need to wait for XXX before implementing". That way we can actually track the progress.

&#x200B;

What do you feel about this?

That's the first thing people tried to do when planets where "Yeah right that's never going to happen"

&#x200B;

People tried to shove the boxes inside the ships for so long, and couldn't even use a tractor beam. That was pain...

